Provision application passwords, and the applications they belong to.

kanidm's application passwords are per-person, per-application credentials for
LDAP/basic-auth clients. Upstream kanidm-provision has no entity for either, so
neither can be declared -- and kanidm itself only MINTS passwords, returning the
cleartext once, which cannot be provisioned because the value would differ on
every run.

This pairs with patches/kanidm/set-application-password.patch, which adds an
optional `secret` to the SCIM create-password endpoint. Without that patch the
`set_application_password` call here will fail, since stock kanidm ignores an
unknown field and mints its own.

State gains two things:

  applications.<name> = { displayName, linkedGroup }
  persons.<name>.applicationPasswords.<label> = { application, secretFile }

The label is the KEY, because a label is what distinguishes two passwords for
the same application -- kanidm's own model, not an invention here.

`secretFile` is a path rather than a value for the same reason `basicSecretFile`
is: the state JSON lands in the nix store and is world-readable.

Ordering is load-bearing and goes groups -> applications -> persons. An
application needs its linked group to exist (only members of that group may hold
a password for it, which is kanidm's rule), and a person's application passwords
need the application.

Passwords are set on every run without checking first. That is safe, and
deliberately so: the server compares the value and does nothing when it already
matches, so there is no local state to track and no way for a run to drift. It
also means this cannot read a password back -- only hashes are stored -- which
is why "set" is the only shape that works.

APPLIES ON TOP OF service-accounts.patch, not on stock v1.3.0. Both touch the
same three files at the same anchors -- the endpoint constants, the methods
before delete_entity, the State struct -- so generated against the pristine tree
this failed five of its hunks when applied second. nixpkgs applies `patches` in
list order, so this one is listed second and its context assumes the first.

